{"rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and a new passage of about 250 words in the text field.", "text": "Mira snatched her hand away as if the page had bitten her. The pencil rolled from her fingers and clicked against the floorboards, yet the scratching went on, steady and unhurried, like someone writing a letter they had composed many times before. Tobias struck the match himself. The flare was small and orange, and in its trembling circle they bent over the notebook together. Beneath her clumsy capitals, in a slanting hand the color of old rust, new words were forming: YOU DID MEAN IT. YOU WROTE MY NAME. Mira's throat went dry. She had written no name. She had copied only what her grandmother left on the first page, a line of strange syllables she had taken for a nursery rhyme. The match burned down to Tobias's fingertips, and he dropped it with a hiss. In the dark, the scratching paused, then resumed. \"What's it saying?\" he whispered. She did not answer. She was counting the knocks that had come before, three, then three again, and remembering how her grandmother always rapped on the kitchen table three times before she said grace. On the porch the boards fell quiet, as though whatever waited there was listening too. Mira found the pencil by feel, pressed the notebook open, and wrote beneath the rusty letters, her hand shaking so badly the words would barely hold together: WHO ARE YOU. The paper warmed again. This time the answer came quickly, only four letters, and Mira knew them before the last stroke was finished. They spelled the name she had called her grandmother as a child."}
